<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meiji era</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Meiji period, Meiji jidai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meiji era was the period of Japanese history from 23 October 1868 to 30 July 1912, the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a>. In it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> moved from an isolated feudal society at risk of colonisation by Western powers to an industrialised nation-state and emerging great power, adopting Western science, technology, law and political ideas; it opened with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a> of 1868 and gave Japan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889. Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> rebelled against the changes in the 1870s, most notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a>'s Satsuma Rebellion. Victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> in 1894–1895 and over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1904–1905 made Japan an international power. It was followed by the Taishō era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meiji-era.jpg" alt="Meiji era" width="330" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uchida Kuichi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meiji_tenno3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1774 Sugita Genpaku and his colleagues published the Kaitai Shinsho, a translation of a Dutch anatomical text, and by the early nineteenth century rangaku scholars had prepared intellectual foundations for the rapid modernisation of the <b>Meiji period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The word first meant a lord or a dear one; from the <b>Meiji era</b> to 1945 the song was understood as wishing the emperor a long reign, and since 1999 the government has read it as a wish for the lasting peace and prosperity of Japan under the…</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Kōjō no Tsuki&quot; (&quot;The Moon over the Ruined Castle&quot;) is a Japanese song of the <b>Meiji period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kojo-no-tsuki">Kōjō no Tsuki</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody has been popular since the <b>Meiji period</b>, and its words took their present form then.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sakura-sakura">Sakura Sakura</a></li></ul></section>
